      "Root-caused a real-world gray-screen bug (reported: logo shows, then blank, no loading animation, reproduced in both the installed PWA and a plain browser tab, so not device-specific) -- after several rapid deploys in a row, a stale service-worker precache (or, absent that, a stale browser-cached index.html) can reference JS/CSS asset filenames that no longer exist on the server post-deploy, since Vite's hashed filenames change every build and old ones aren't retained. registerType stays 'prompt' (unchanged, deliberate product requirement -- see 1.12.7's comment -- never force a reload mid-workout), so this is fixed at three layers instead of by changing that update-prompt behavior:",
      "New vercel.json (didn't exist before): explicit Cache-Control headers, since none were previously set and Vercel's defaults aren't guaranteed cache-safe for an SPA app shell. '/', '/index.html', '/manifest.webmanifest', '/sw.js', and '/workbox-*.js' all get 'no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate' -- these must always be revalidated against the server, never served stale from any cache layer. '/assets/*' (Vite's content-hashed JS/CSS) gets 'public, max-age=31536000, immutable' -- safe to cache forever since a content change always produces a new filename.",
      "vite.config.js workbox options gained cleanupOutdatedCaches: true (purges old precache storage once a new service worker activates, instead of potentially accumulating/serving from stale versions), clientsClaim: true (once an update IS applied -- still only ever via the existing explicit 'Reload now' flow, this doesn't touch when that happens -- the new worker claims every open tab immediately instead of requiring a second navigation), and an explicit navigateFallback: '/index.html' (was previously left to generateSW's default inference). Verified all three actually landed in the generated dist/sw.js (clientsClaim(), cleanupOutdatedCaches(), and a NavigationRoute bound to index.html all present).",
      "New self-heal inline script in index.html's <head>, ahead of the app's module bundle script tag specifically so it's already listening even if that bundle itself is what fails to load (a handler defined inside the bundle can't run if the bundle never loaded). Listens for (1) a <script>/<link> tag failing to load, capturing-phase 'error' on window, which does fire for resource load failures despite not bubbling, and (2) an unhandledrejection whose message matches known dynamic-import/chunk-load failure signatures ('Failed to fetch dynamically imported module', 'Loading chunk ... failed', etc, the standard shapes Vite/Workbox module-loading failures present as). On either, clears every Cache Storage entry for the origin (drops any stale service-worker precache) and reloads exactly once, guarded by a sessionStorage flag (dl_stale_asset_reload_attempted) so a genuine reload-won't-fix-it error can't trap someone in a loop -- tries once per tab session, then gets out of the way and lets the real error surface normally. This is the layer that actually guarantees no user hits this again regardless of root cause: it doesn't require diagnosing exactly why an asset failed to load, only that one did, and self-heals either way.",
      "Auth.jsx: fixed the 'Continue with Google' button being unreachable on short mobile viewports. Root cause: the form section was a flex column with justifyContent: 'flex-end' inside an overflowY:auto container -- a known CSS trap where, once content is taller than the container, flex-end alignment anchors the *end* of the content and the overflow happens at the *start*, which browsers don't always make reachable via normal scrolling (an 'unsafe' alignment in flexbox terms). Since the Google button sits near the top of that block, it could become genuinely unreachable rather than just requiring a scroll. Fixed by dropping justifyContent: 'flex-end' entirely (content now flows normally top-to-bottom, so overflow -- if any -- happens safely at the bottom, which normal downward scrolling always reaches) and swapping the two sections' flex-grow weights (logo block 1.3->1, form block 1->1.3) so the form gets more of the available height on short screens to begin with.",
      "New migration_071_user_preferences.sql + lib/prefs.js sync layer: preferences (everything getPrefs()/setPref() manage -- units, Muscle Names mode, Home dashboard layout, rest timer defaults, etc.) previously lived only in localStorage, so clearing browser data (including as the fix for the gray-screen bug above) silently wiped every preference with no way to recover them. New user_preferences table (user_id primary key referencing auth.users with on delete cascade, matching the rest of the schema's account-deletion pattern; one jsonb blob per user rather than a normalized table, so it doesn't need a schema migration every time DEFAULTS gains a new key) backs them up. lib/prefs.js imports supabaseClient directly (not queries.js, to avoid a circular import since queries.js already imports getPrefs from this file) and exports initPrefsSync(userId) (called once at sign-in from App.jsx: pulls the server copy and merges it UNDER whatever's already in localStorage, so local always wins for any key it actually has and server only fills in what's missing -- a no-op on a normal browser, a full restore on a freshly-cleared one) and clearPrefsSync() (called at sign-out, so a stray debounced write can never land under the wrong user_id if a different person signs in on the same device). setPref() now schedules a 1.5s-debounced full-snapshot push to the server on every call, transparent to every existing call site.",
