      "Home.jsx: new activeWorkout/onResumeWorkout props. The bottom CTA now shows 'Resume Workout' (calls onResumeWorkout, which sets mode to 'workout' without touching resumeWorkout) instead of 'Start Workout' whenever App's resumeWorkout state is populated -- previously Home was never shown at all while a workout was active, so this case didn't exist. onStartWorkout is unchanged and still nulls resumeWorkout, since it's explicitly for starting fresh.",
      "lib/whatsNext.js: removed the three items shipped this version (exercise notes/setup editing outside a workout, quick path to the main menu from the workout screen, exercise-specific library charts). Body heat map remains, still unshipped.",
      "ExportWorkoutModal.jsx: fixed the reported flickering/glitching during \"Save image\" generation, confirmed to happen specifically during html2canvas's capture (not on open or while toggling) and to self-resolve after roughly 10 seconds. The scroll-position fix shipped earlier this version (see below) was real but not the culprit here -- the actual cause was the progress-photo Story background: it's a Supabase signed URL, loaded cross-origin via crossOrigin=\"anonymous\" so html2canvas's capture doesn't taint the canvas, and when that live cross-origin fetch stalled (flaky network, CORS quirk on the signed URL), html2canvas sat waiting on its imageTimeout before giving up -- the ~10s hang. Fix: pre-fetch the photo and convert it to a same-origin data URL as soon as the modal opens (well before Save Image is tapped), and use that for the `<img>` background instead of the live remote URL. The capture then never needs a network fetch at all, so there's nothing left to hang on. Falls back to the original remote-URL + crossOrigin path if the conversion fails, now with imageTimeout capped at 3000ms instead of html2canvas's ~15s default so that fallback path can't stall nearly as long either.",
      "ExportWorkoutModal.jsx: also fixed a real (smaller) issue found while investigating -- previewRef sits inside a scrollable ancestor (the modal's own overflowY:auto content div), and html2canvas clones the DOM off-screen to render it, which doesn't carry over live scroll position. A scrolled ancestor came back scrollTop 0 in the clone, out of sync with where the live page measured the preview to be. Reset the scrollable container to scrollTop 0 immediately before calling html2canvas, and pass scrollX: 0, scrollY: 0 so html2canvas doesn't layer its own window-scroll offset on top of that.",
      "WorkoutHistory.jsx: found the actual root cause after the two fixes above didn't resolve it -- confirmed via user report to only happen with a progress photo attached, and only in Workout History (not the post-workout summary). html2canvas clones the *entire* document, not just the target element, to preserve computed styles, and waits for every image in that clone to finish loading before it can render anything -- including ProgressPhotoBlock's own on-page photo thumbnail and full-resolution viewer, which sit completely outside previewRef and were never touched by the data-URL prefetch fix above. That separate image (private signed URL, real photo file size, likely uncached) was almost certainly what was actually stalling for ~10s. Added data-html2canvas-ignore=\"true\" to both of ProgressPhotoBlock's <img> elements (the thumbnail and the full-screen viewer) -- html2canvas honors this attribute natively and excludes the element from the clone/wait entirely, and neither image was ever part of the captured preview to begin with. ExerciseThumb's per-exercise <img> elements (exercise-media, a public bucket) weren't touched since they aren't implicated by the no-photo/has-photo split in the report, and also aren't part of the preview.",
      "Still reproduced on Android Chrome/PWA specifically (not desktop) after the ignore-attribute fix above, which pointed at raw clone/style-computation cost rather than any single image: html2canvas's DocumentCloner clones starting from document.documentElement regardless of data-html2canvas-ignore on individual images, walking and copying computed styles for the *entire live app tree* -- Home's full DOM (calendar, insight cards, any open admin panels) stays mounted, just visually covered, behind WorkoutHistory's own DetailView (all of that workout's exercises, sets, and edit UI) -- and that whole-tree clone is what was genuinely slow to compute synchronously on a mid-range Android device, not fast enough anywhere near desktop to be visible there, which is why it never reproduced off-device. Root fix: index.html's #root div now carries data-html2canvas-ignore=\"true\" permanently, and ExportWorkoutModal.jsx renders through a createPortal(..., document.body) instead of as a normal descendant -- the modal now lives as a *sibling* of #root rather than inside it, so html2canvas's clone walk skips the entire live app in one step (DocumentCloner checks the ignore attribute per-node during cloning itself, before recursing into children, so this also cuts the traversal, not just the final render) and only has the modal's own small subtree left to clone. The two attribute-level image fixes above are now redundant (their subtree is skipped regardless) but harmless, so left in place.",
